**Design Justification Calculations for a Dual-Sensor Hybrid Seismic Alert System**

EEE383 Electronic System Design — Group 01, Section 01

**I. Signal Conditioning Op-Amp (LM358N) — Component Derivation**

**A. Stage 1 — Inverting Charge-Amplifier Gain**

**Given:  **Rin = 10 kΩ, Rf = 100 kΩ (Subsystem 3.3 component list).

	Av = -RfRin	(1)

**Substitution:  **Av = -100 kΩ10 kΩ

**Result:  **Av = -10

**B. Stage 2 — Sallen-Key Low-Pass Filter**

**Given:  **target cutoff fc ≈ 15 Hz (Subsystem 3.3 objective); R chosen = 100 kΩ to reuse the Stage-1 feedback value.

	C = 12π R fc	(2)

**Substitution:  **C = 12π × 100,000 × 15

**Result:  **C = 1.061 × 10- 7 F = 106.1 nF — nearest standard value 100 nF

**Verification:  **with the standard values R = 100 kΩ, C = 100 nF (×2 each):

	fc = 12π R C	(3)

**Substitution:  **fc = 12π × 100,000 × 100 × 10- 9

**Result:  **fc = 15.92 Hz

**C. Reference (Bias) Voltage Verification**

**Given:  **FILT_OUT swings between 0.834 V and 0.935 V, no clipping (Proteus DAT export, Subsystem 3.3 simulation).

	VREF = Vmin + Vmax2	(4)

**Substitution:  **VREF = 0.834 + 0.9352

**Result:  **VREF = 0.8845 V ≈ 0.889 V

The bias point is not an independently chosen value — with the piezo input AC-coupled through the 10 µF capacitor, no DC path exists through Rin, so the amplifier's DC output is forced to equal the non-inverting bias voltage. Averaging the observed swing bounds reproduces the same ~0.889 V bias, confirming the stage is centered with symmetric headroom on both rails.

**II. Python/****ObsPy****-Derived Earthquake Signal**

**Given:  **ObsPy-retrieved BW.RJOB seismogram exported as quake_signal.txt for the Proteus File Generator; N = 850 samples, T = 8.5 s.

	fs = NT	(5)

**Substitution:  **fs = 8508.5

**Result:  **fs = 100 Hz

	fNyquist = fs2	(6)

**Substitution:  **fNyquist = 1002

**Result:  **fNyquist = 50 Hz

50 Hz matches the firmware's 100 Hz FFT sampling rate (Subsystem 3.4) and comfortably exceeds the 1–10 Hz seismic band of interest.

**III. Synthetic Noise Signals for False-Positive Validation**

Each synthetic vibration signal is converted from a published peak particle velocity (PPV) to peak acceleration using:

	apeak = vpeak × 2π f	(7)

**A. Construction / Pile-Driving Vibration (Continuous)**

**Given:  **PPVref = 0.644 in/s, typical impact pile driver at 25 ft reference distance [1]; dominant band 3–12 Hz for pile driving [2], 5–30 Hz for construction equipment generally [3].

**Chosen:  **synthetic test frequency f = 10 Hz (upper edge of the 1–10 Hz seismic detection band).

**Substitution:  **vpeak = 0.644 × 0.0254 = 0.01636 m/s;  apeak = 0.01636 × 2π × 10

**Result:  **apeak = 1.028 m/s² = 0.105 g

	y(t) = 0.105 g · sin(2π · 10 · t)	(8)

**B. Door-Slam Vibration (Transient)**

**Given:  **PPV range 0.15–1.9 in/s for door slam [1]; discriminating detectors reject door-slam energy by blocking above 12 Hz [4].

**Chosen:  **synthetic test frequency f = 20 Hz (above the 12 Hz discriminator threshold, outside the 1–10 Hz band).

	vpeak = PPVmin + PPVmax2	(9)

**Substitution:  **vpeak = 0.15 + 1.92 = 1.025 in/s = 0.02604 m/s

**Result:  **apeak = 0.02604 × 2π × 20 = 3.272 m/s² = 0.334 g

	y(t) = 0.334 g · e-t⁄τ · sin(2π · 20 · t),  τ = 0.05 s	(10)

0.334 g exceeds the 0.3 g accelerometer threshold on raw amplitude, but its 20 Hz content lies outside the 1–10 Hz FFT seismic band, so the frequency-domain criterion rejects it — confirming that amplitude thresholding alone is insufficient and frequency-domain classification is required.

**C. Train-Induced Ground Vibration (Continuous)**

**Given:  **PPV 0.084–0.229 mm/s (near-field monitoring point, 100–300 km/h) and characteristic frequency 15.18 ± 0.10 Hz, repeatable across train types and speeds [5].

**Chosen:  **representative PPV = 0.157 mm/s (midpoint), f = 15.18 Hz.

**Substitution:  **vpeak = 0.157 × 10- 3 = 1.57 × 10- 4 m/s;  apeak = 1.57 × 10- 4 × 2π × 15.18

**Result:  **apeak = 0.0150 m/s² = 0.00153 g

	y(t) = 0.00153 g · sin(2π · 15.18 · t)	(11)

Both the amplitude (≪ 0.3 g) and the 15.18 Hz frequency (outside the 1–10 Hz seismic band) place train vibration well clear of the alarm criteria at typical urban monitoring distances — an “easy-reject” case, unlike construction/pile-driving.

**D. Proteus File-Generator Setup (Consistency Check)**

	N = fs × T	(12)

**Given:  **fₛ = 100 Hz (matches Eq. 5, the earthquake-file sample rate); T = 8.5 s (matches quake_signal.txt duration, for direct side-by-side false-positive testing).

**Substitution:  **N = 100 × 8.5

**Result:  **N = 850 samples

N = 850 matches quake_signal.txt exactly (850 samples, 8.5 s) — confirmed consistent, so all three synthetic noise files (construction, door-slam, train) import into the same Proteus File Generator model and ADC sample-and-hold timing as the real earthquake test vector.

**IV. Sensing-Node Maximum Power Calculation**

**Given:  **Vrail = 3.3 V.

**I₁ — ESP32-S3-WROOM-1:  **802.11b, 1 Mbps, @20.5 dBm, peak active TX current, Table 6-4 [6].

**Result:  **I1 = 355 mA

**I₂ — MPU-6050:  **gyroscope + accelerometer + DMP enabled, normal operating current [7].

**Result:  **I2 = 3.9 mA

**I₃ — LM358N:  **I_S max, V+ = 5 V, both amplifiers combined [8].

**Result:  **I3 = 1.2 mA

**I₄ — RBD-2229 piezo module:  **onboard bias resistor ≈ 1 MΩ from the 3.3 V rail 

	I4 = VR	(13)

**Substitution:  **I4 = 3.31,000,000

**Result:  **I4 = 3.3 µA

	Itotal = I1 + I2 + I3 + I4	(14)

**Substitution:  **Itotal = 355 + 3.9 + 1.2 + 0.0033

**Result:  **Itotal = 360.1 mA

	Ptotal = Itotal × Vrail	(15)

**Substitution:  **Ptotal = 0.3601 × 3.3

**Result:  **Ptotal = 1.188 W

**Given:  **F1 polyfuse hold current = 500 mA (RXEF-class).

	Margin = Ihold - Itotal	(16)

**Substitution:  **Margin = 500 - 360.1

**Result:  **Margin = 139.9 mA (28% headroom, steady-state worst case)

**V. PCB Trace Width Calculation (IPC-2221)**

**Given:  **I_design = 1 A (TP4056 module charge current, Subsystem 3.1); external layer, 1 oz copper, W = 20 mil, ΔT = 10°C; k = 0.048, b = 0.44, c = 0.725 [9]; t = 1.378 mil (1 oz copper thickness).

	A = W × t	(17)

**Substitution:  **A = 20 × 1.378

**Result:  **A = 27.56 mil²

	Imax = k · ΔTb · Ac	(18)

**Substitution:  **Imax = 0.048 × 100.44 × 27.560.725 = 0.048 × 2.754 × 11.07

**Result:  **Imax = 1.463 A

	Margin = Imax - Idesign	(19)

**Substitution:  **Margin = 1.463 - 1

**Result:  **Margin = 0.463 A (46% headroom) — 20 mil / 1 oz external trace confirmed adequate

